<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sergo Ordzhonikidze</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1886">1886</a>–<a href="/years/1937">1937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Grigory Ordzhonikidze</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sergo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladikavkaz">Ordzhonikidze</a> was a Georgian Bolshevik and close ally of Stalin who directed the Soviet conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, including the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in 1921. His harsh treatment of Georgian communists who opposed the Transcaucasian federation sparked the Georgian Affair of 1922. He later ran Soviet heavy industry and died in 1937, officially of heart failure but probably by suicide.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sergo-ordzhonikidze.jpg" alt="Sergo Ordzhonikidze" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: G.K. Ordzhonikidze · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sergo_Ordzhonikidze_in_Uniform.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sergo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladikavkaz">Ordzhonikidze</a> was born Grigol Ordzhonikidze on 24 October 1886 in Ghoresha, a village in the Kutais Governorate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, now in the Imereti region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. His father Konstantine belonged to an impoverished noble family and his mother Eupraxia was a peasant; she died six weeks after his birth. He was raised by an uncle and aunt, and his father died when he was ten. He trained as a medical orderly and briefly worked as a medical assistant.</p><p>He joined the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party in 1903, at 17, and worked in an underground printshop for the Bolshevik faction. He was first arrested in December 1905 for transporting arms, worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>, and was imprisoned several times by the Russian police. He was in Siberian exile when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a> began in 1917, then returned and took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>During the Civil War he was the leading Bolshevik in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. In 1918 he persuaded Chechen and Ingush leaders that the soviet system resembled the Islamic order they favoured, and with Ingush help the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladikavkaz">Vladikavkaz</a>. After Lenin ordered him on 17 March 1920 to prepare an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, he oversaw the invasions of Azerbaijan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. As head of the Kavbiuro he clashed with the Georgian Bolsheviks, especially Budu Mdivani, whose advice he ignored. He backed their union in the Transcaucasian Socialist Federative Soviet Republic and was its First Secretary until 1926. Colleagues called him authoritarian; Lenin explained that he shouted because he was deaf in one ear.</p><p>In 1926 Stalin made him head of the Workers' and Peasants' Inspectorate and the Central Control Commission. In 1930 he took over Vesenkha, reorganised in 1932 as the People's Commissariat of Heavy Industry, and became a full member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a>. He argued Stalin down to a yearly industrial growth target of 13 to 14 per cent for the second five-year plan and helped launch the Stakhanovite movement in 1935. He tried to protect his officials from the hunt for wreckers and saboteurs and had prosecutors barred from factories, which strained his friendship with Stalin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladikavkaz">Ordzhonikidze</a> died on 18 February 1937, a few days before a Central Committee plenum at which he was expected to speak on wrecking in his commissariat. Rumours spread at once that Stalin had ordered his death or driven him to suicide. The historian Oleg Khlevniuk thinks suicide likely, and the cause remains contested. His brother Papulia was shot in November 1937, and other relatives and colleagues were arrested. Vladikavkaz was renamed Ordzhonikidze in 1931 and bore the name again from 1954 to 1990.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sergo Ordzhonikidze” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Georgian communists who opposed it lost out to Stalin and <b>Ordzhonikidze</b> in the so-called Georgian Affair.</blockquote><a href="/state/transcaucasian-sfsr">Transcaucasian SFSR</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ghoresha, in what is now <a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.08,43.27&amp;year=1919" aria-label="Ghoresha on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5601 666 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sergo Ordzhonikidze"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6051 936h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6051 936h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.08,43.27&amp;year=1919">What was here in 1919? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
